NAME
       chcon - change the SELinux context of files (until the next relabel)

SYNOPSIS
       chcon [-R] [-v] [-h] [-u USER] [-r ROLE] [-t TYPE] [-l RANGE] FILE...
       chcon [-R] [-v] CONTEXT FILE...
       chcon [-R] [-v] --reference=RFILE FILE...

DESCRIPTION
       Every file has an SELinux context of four parts, user:role:type:level,
       for example system_u:object_r:httpd_sys_content_t:s0. The type is the
       part the targeted policy looks at: a web server (httpd_t) may read
       files of type httpd_sys_content_t, but not files of type
       admin_home_t or user_home_t.

       chcon writes a new context straight onto the files. The change is
       real at once, but it is not remembered anywhere: restorecon, a full
       relabel (touch /.autorelabel and reboot) or fixfiles puts the label
       back to what the policy's file-context rules say.

       For a change that must last, add a rule with semanage fcontext and
       apply it with restorecon. Use chcon for a quick test, or not at all.

       Show contexts with ls -Z (files) and ps -Z (processes).

OPTIONS
       -t TYPE, --type=TYPE
              Set only the type part. This is the option you will use.

       -u USER, --user=USER
              Set the SELinux user part.

       -r ROLE, --role=ROLE
              Set the role part.

       -l RANGE, --range=RANGE
              Set the level (MLS/MCS range) part.

       --reference=RFILE
              Copy the context of RFILE.

       -R, --recursive
              Change directories and everything in them.

       -h, --no-dereference
              Change a symbolic link itself, not the file it points to.

       -v, --verbose
              Report every file processed.

EXAMPLES
       Let the web server read a directory, for a test:

           chcon -R -t httpd_sys_content_t /srv/web
           ls -Zd /srv/web

       Give a file the same label as the default web page directory:

           chcon --reference=/var/www/html /srv/web/index.html

       The lasting way to do the same thing:

           semanage fcontext -a -t httpd_sys_content_t '/srv/web(/.*)?'
           restorecon -Rv /srv/web

NOTES
       On the exam, a context set only with chcon is lost when the graders
       relabel or restore the files. Always use semanage fcontext plus
       restorecon when a task asks for a context.

SEE ALSO
       restorecon(8), semanage(8), ls(1), ps(1), selinux(8)
